\chapter{Api}\label{chap:api}

The Api concept is the surface a person touches. For the walking skeleton it
is a single \texttt{main} that tells the whole story: open the demo accounts,
post the demo transaction, print the balances, and then --- the proof that the
invariant is real --- try to post an \emph{unbalanced} transaction and show it
is refused. Api depends on both other concepts; nothing depends on Api.

\section{The walking skeleton}

\texttt{main} composes the two seed snippets its neighbours own, then reports.
After the balanced posting, \texttt{cash} holds \$100.00 and \texttt{revenue}
holds \(-\)\$100.00; their sum is zero, which is the invariant made visible on
the last line. The deliberately unbalanced transaction (a lone \$5.00 debit)
must be rejected --- if it were ever accepted, the final sum would drift from
zero and the program would say so.

@d Api: main @{#include <stdio.h>
#include "accounts.h"
#include "ledger.h"

int main(void) {
    @<Accounts: demo accounts@>
    @<Ledger: demo posting@>

    printf("posted transaction: %s\n", rc == 0 ? "OK" : "REJECTED");
    printf("  %-8s %8.2f\n", acc_name(cash),    acc_balance(cash)    / 100.0);
    printf("  %-8s %8.2f\n", acc_name(revenue), acc_balance(revenue) / 100.0);

    /* The invariant, demonstrated: an unbalanced transaction is refused. */
    Entry bad[] = { { cash, +500 } };                 /* sums to 500, not zero */
    printf("unbalanced transaction: %s\n",
           ledger_post(bad, 1) == 0 ? "ACCEPTED (bug!)" : "REJECTED (correct)");

    long total = acc_balance(cash) + acc_balance(revenue);
    printf("sum of all balances: %ld cents (must be 0)\n", total);
    return 0;
}
@}
